tablished established in china  as um  as in western countries  joe has said she will appeal the court's  decision  when she does interest is likely to be  great  both in and outside of china  yachu wang is a china researcher at  human rights watch and joins me now from  new york ms wang are you surprised that  the case against jujun was thrown out  well i don't think i am surprised  because the court has already stole  uh  uh prevented uh uh shenzhen from you  know submitting uh uh  more evidence from uh you know gathering  evidence uh from uh camera and things so  there are already indications that the  case wasn't in a good shape  now you're talking about evidence such  as uh the security camera footage of the  workplace where this harassment took  place and then the accused juju not even  being required to come to court to try  and testify i mean therefore for me the  question really is do you think china's  legal system is tilted against women who  make complaints about sexual harassment  i absolutely i think so  uh you know why shouldn't jujin be  somebody to testify in the court uh you  know is in clear indication that you  know it's very very hard for women to uh  sue for sexual harassment if the court  system doesn't you know put a weight on  personal accounts on personal  testimonies and there are any  other things that in the system that  make it's very hard for women to you  know go through the process and win the  case but why is that the case in china  well i think you know it's just like in  any other countries it's a patriarchal  countries that you know it's harder for  women to  uh you know uh  gain  you know redress through the court  system because well i mean just like in  many other countries the laws are  written by  men and you know the judge is a man and  the lawyer is a man  but at the same time you have  the same country china which also passed  sexual harassment legislation last year  last month well-known singer chris wu  was arrested on 